        "math_academy_id": "q-110308",
        "problem": "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{\u2032}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ at some points. Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(3)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-5$ |",
        "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)={g}^{\u2032}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(3)={g}^{\u2032}(3){f}^{-1}(3)+\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(3))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=-3,$ ${g}^{\u2032}(3)=1,$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(-2)=-4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(3) & ={g}^{\u2032}(3){f}^{-1}(3)+\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{-3}{{f}^{\u2032}(-2)} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{(-3)}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
